The java virtual machine — what's actually running.

You have spent nine modules writing Java, the language. This final module is about the machine that runs it — because Java is really two things: a language, and the java virtual machine that executes it. Understanding the java virtual machine explains Java's famous portability, its surprising speed, and a whole family of tools — garbage collection, class loading, reflection — that only make sense once you can see the machine underneath. Think of this section as the map for the rest of the module.

Start with what happens to your code. A C program compiles straight to instructions for one kind of CPU. Java takes a detour, and that detour is the key to everything. The java compiler compiles your source not to machine code, but to bytecode — a compact, CPU-independent instruction set, stored in dot-class files. That bytecode doesn't run on your hardware directly; it runs on the java virtual machine, a program that reads bytecode and executes it. Ship the same class file, or jar, to Windows, Linux, or a Mac, and each platform's java virtual machine runs it unchanged. That is write once, run anywhere: the virtual machine is the portable layer that absorbs the differences between machines.

But the java virtual machine is not just an interpreter. It's a small runtime that continuously does four jobs while your program runs. First, it loads classes on demand, and verifies their bytecode is safe before running it. Second, it executes that bytecode — interpreting at first, then handing the hot spots to the just-in-time compiler, which compiles frequently-run methods to native machine code while the program is running. Third, it manages memory — allocating objects on the heap, and reclaiming dead ones with the garbage collector, so you never call free. And fourth, it provides the runtime services the language leans on: threads, security checks, reflection. That just-in-time compiler is why so-called interpreted Java is fast — a long-running method ends up as optimized native code, sometimes tuned to how the program actually behaves at runtime, which an ahead-of-time compiler can't do.

Now, three names that often get muddled: J-D-K, J-R-E, and java virtual machine. The picture is simply three nested boxes. The java virtual machine is the execution engine that runs bytecode — the innermost piece. The java runtime environment is the virtual machine plus the standard class libraries — enough to run Java. And the java development kit is the runtime environment plus the development tools: the compiler, the debugger, the jar tool, the documentation generator — enough to build Java. So you install a development kit to develop; a user only needs the runtime environment to run.

And here's a quiet consequence worth noticing. The java virtual machine runs bytecode — not Java specifically. Any language that compiles to valid class files runs on it, which is why Kotlin, Scala, Clojure, and Groovy all target the virtual machine, reuse its libraries, and share its garbage collector and just-in-time compiler. The java virtual machine has become a general-purpose runtime platform, and Java is its first and most common language. With that map in hand, the module now zooms into the machine's parts — starting with the one that runs constantly, and invisibly: the garbage collector.
